1
00:00:00,067 --> 00:00:00,756
Olive drab.

2
00:00:00,796 --> 00:00:07,789
It's the color on almost every American bomb and artillery shell, and on its own, it means almost nothing.

3
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:09,443
It's the base coat.

4
00:00:09,483 --> 00:00:17,455
Under the military's ammunition color code, MIL-STD-709, the real message is painted on top, as a stripe.

5
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:21,420
That code covers ammunition twenty millimeters and larger.

6
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:24,921
Smaller bullets use a different system, a painted tip.

7
00:00:25,433 --> 00:00:25,838
Yellow.

8
00:00:25,878 --> 00:00:28,060
A yellow band means high explosive.

9
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:37,321
A live Mark eighty-two, the five hundred pound general purpose bomb, is olive drab with a yellow band about three inches wide around the nose.

10
00:00:37,567 --> 00:00:41,926
Bombs with a heat resistant thermal coating get two yellow bands.

11
00:00:41,966 --> 00:00:45,310
One stripe, and everyone on the flight line knows.

12
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:46,461
This one is real.

13
00:00:46,967 --> 00:00:47,353
Brown.

14
00:00:47,393 --> 00:00:49,200
Brown means low explosive.

15
00:00:49,433 --> 00:00:51,065
Not the blast, the push.

16
00:00:51,105 --> 00:00:53,643
Propellant charges and rocket motors.

17
00:00:53,683 --> 00:00:57,404
A low explosive burns very fast instead of detonating.

18
00:00:57,633 --> 00:01:04,107
So a brown band on a missile tells the crew loading it that the motor is live, even if the warhead isn't.

19
00:01:04,633 --> 00:01:05,283
Light blue.

20
00:01:05,323 --> 00:01:06,912
Light blue means practice.

21
00:01:07,167 --> 00:01:14,194
The B D U thirty-three is a blue, twenty-five pound practice bomb built to fly like a Mark eighty-two.

22
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:16,471
But blue does not mean harmless.

23
00:01:16,511 --> 00:01:22,250
Inside is a spotting charge that throws up a puff of smoke, so crews can score their hit.

24
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:29,813
There are hundreds of thousands of them on American bombing ranges, and the Air Force's advice if you find one is simple.

25
00:01:29,853 --> 00:01:30,846
Don't pick it up.

26
00:01:31,367 --> 00:01:32,164
Light green.

27
00:01:32,204 --> 00:01:33,840
Light green means smoke.

28
00:01:34,067 --> 00:01:36,970
Screening smoke, to hide troops and vehicles.

29
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:40,763
Or marking smoke, to point out a target or a landing zone.

30
00:01:40,803 --> 00:01:44,426
This round is built to hide things, not to destroy them.

31
00:01:44,933 --> 00:01:45,272
White.

32
00:01:45,312 --> 00:01:46,847
White means illumination.

33
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:49,613
These rounds aren't meant to hit the ground.

34
00:01:49,653 --> 00:01:56,403
High above the battlefield, the shell releases a flare that drifts down under a parachute and turns the night bright.

35
00:01:56,933 --> 00:01:57,244
Black.

36
00:01:57,284 --> 00:01:58,878
Black means armor defeating.

37
00:01:59,133 --> 00:02:02,072
These are the rounds built to punch through tanks.

38
00:02:02,112 --> 00:02:05,885
It's the same idea as the black tip on an armor piercing bullet.

39
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:07,008
And gray.

40
00:02:07,048 --> 00:02:09,599
Gray is the one nobody wants to see.

41
00:02:09,639 --> 00:02:11,542
A gray body means chemical.

42
00:02:11,767 --> 00:02:14,022
The band tells you which kind.

43
00:02:14,062 --> 00:02:17,695
Dark red is a riot control agent, like tear gas.

44
00:02:17,735 --> 00:02:20,297
Violet is an incapacitating agent.

45
00:02:20,337 --> 00:02:22,669
Dark green is a toxic chemical.

46
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:29,240
And if that dark green band is broken into half inch dashes, it marks a binary nerve agent.

47
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:33,938
Two chemicals stored apart, that only mix after the round is fired.

48
00:02:34,467 --> 00:02:40,819
Colors fade, and other countries use their own codes, so a color is never proof that something is safe.

49
00:02:41,067 --> 00:02:53,380
But on an American flight line, yellow is the blast, brown is the push, blue is practice, green is smoke, white is light, black is armor, and gray is the one you hope never leaves the bunker.

50
00:02:53,633 --> 00:02:55,561
Which stripe surprised you the most?

51
00:02:55,601 --> 00:02:56,873
Tell me in the comments.
